Confirmed. The stale comment at lines 429–435 says the unknown-type validation "belongs to the quote path" — which is now precise and correct, so that one survives. But two others are genuinely stale.

**Finding 1 (line 434):** the comment on `payoutForIncident` says "The third validation the spec calls for (unknown item type) belongs to the quote path". That was written when unknown types were silently zero-specced. It is still true — unknown types are rejected at `specForItem`, reached only from the quote path — so I leave it.

**Finding 2 (lines 213–216):** this is the stale one:

```ts
// All amounts are rounded to whole G in the MHPCO's favour. For a premium —
// money flowing to the MHPCO — its favour is upwards. The payout half of this
// rule rounds the other way, but no test drives payouts yet, so only the
// premium direction is named here.
```

"no test drives payouts yet" is false — payouts are driven by many of the 42 passing tests (`payoutForDamage`, `payoutForIncident`, `runClaim` all exist). What is actually still pending is payout *rounding*. The comment misstates which fact is unproven, which is exactly the failure mode a comment like this exists to prevent.

Let me apply the fix — one change, Rule 2 (reveals intent).

